00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:59.860
<v Mike>I'd say part of like, it's mostly like, it's gotta be subsidized by some blood sport.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:02.500
<v Mike>So I think, I think it actually makes sense in the world.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:11.360
<v Mike>Now in the, uh, computer versions of the game, there is a slight drift to the camera as you're turning.

00:11:11.480 --> 00:11:15.120
<v Mike>It does not stay locked on, um, the player.

00:11:15.260 --> 00:11:17.360
<v Sean>And there are two ways to think about this.

00:11:17.580 --> 00:11:24.080
<v Mike>One is, wow, that's a great way to convince, uh, to convey a sense of speed in the game.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:30.360
<v Mike>And then the other one being, wow, that's an incredibly frustrating way to like kind of lose sight of myself for a half a second,

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:33.200
<v Mike>just to have the camera get some movement on it.

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:37.640
<v Mike>And I suppose you need to see it slash play it to believe it.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:44.920
<v Mike>But I genuinely couldn't, um, come to a decision on my own, uh, seeing the two versions side by side.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:57.060
<v Sean>I think that the problem with both is that it still just feels like a very smooth racing experience just with, just with the way that the, the viewpoint of the camera was just was decided and fixed upon.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:08.240
